    "description": "Urban public services connect everyday neighborhood conditions with environmental management, public participation, and resilience in Iranian cities. Waste management research and national urban policy documents show why service quality depends on coordination and maintenance as well as infrastructure. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Urban services", "Municipal management", "Waste management", "Public space", "Urban resilience", "Citizen participation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:33:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:33:44Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Urban Public Services in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore waste management, green spaces, emergency preparedness, street lighting, and citizen services through Iran’s urban policy and local evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "everyday-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The city as an everyday service",
        "content": "Urban public services are experienced through ordinary encounters: a collection service, a usable public space, or a response to a neighborhood problem. For Iran, the national urban policy discussion places these experiences within a wider challenge of coordination. The National Urban Policy and Smart City Strategy summary, prepared through cooperation between UN-Habitat and the Ministry of Roads and Urban Development, identifies weak links between national and local levels, fragmented planning, and limited public participation among the problems it seeks to address. [s1]\n\nThis branch brings together waste management, urban green spaces, fire services, crisis management, street lighting, and citizen service systems. Their inclusion provides an editorial map of the subject; it does not imply that a single organization delivers them all or that service arrangements are identical across cities. The policy document’s emphasis on institutional integration is a useful starting point, but current responsibilities require separate verification.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "waste-management",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Waste collection is one stage of a larger system",
        "content": "Research on Tehran’s food waste illustrates why removing material from streets is only part of service provision. A study published in 2025 used expert consultation and a strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats analysis to develop management strategies. It discusses difficulties associated with mixed waste, the quality of material available for composting, and constraints in processing. These are findings and assessments concerning Tehran, rather than a national measurement of municipal performance. [s2]\n\nThe study also draws attention to people working with waste, including informal recyclers, and to hazards associated with collection, separation, and transport. A cleaner visible environment does not by itself demonstrate safe working conditions or effective treatment. Its recommendations emphasize participation and organizational improvement, making waste management a social and institutional issue alongside a technical one. Because this is a strategy study, its recommendations should not be reported as completed reforms. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "local-participation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation at settlement scale",
        "content": "A more localized example comes from Semnan refugee settlement. In a report published in July 2025, the United Nations Industrial Development Organization described training residents in home-scale vermicomposting: using worms to help transform organic waste into a soil amendment. The activity formed part of a settlement sustainability initiative supported by the Republic of Korea. It offers a concrete example of environmental education linked to household practices. [s3]\n\nThe report establishes that an activity was organized and describes what participants learned. It does not provide a measured annual reduction in waste disposal, evidence of sustained household adoption, or a controlled assessment of environmental outcomes. This distinction preserves the value of the example without turning a training event into proof of a citywide transformation. Settlement projects also need to be understood in their own institutional and social context rather than generalized to every Iranian municipality. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "green-space-and-resilience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Public space, environmental quality, and resilience",
        "content": "UN-Habitat’s Iran country programme outline for 2023–2027 links urban environmental work with natural heritage, municipal waste management, air quality, climate adaptation, and disaster resilience. It explicitly recognizes vulnerable groups, including children, older people, and people with disabilities. These are programme priorities and proposed areas of support, rather than a verified inventory of services delivered in each city. [s4]\n\nThat framework helps explain why green spaces and emergency preparedness belong in the same broad discussion as routine maintenance. Urban environmental quality and the ability to withstand disruption are connected planning concerns. However, the evidence inspected here does not establish current fire-service coverage, emergency response times, lighting reliability, or the accessibility of neighborhood parks. Those subjects require operational records and local studies before meaningful comparisons can be made. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "digital-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Citizen services need more than a digital interface",
        "content": "Iran’s smart city strategy summary identifies participation, cooperation between sectors, data transparency, and unequal access to digital services as issues for policy. Its significance is broader than adopting new equipment: a digital service must be considered alongside who can use it and how institutions respond. These are proposed directions in a planning document, not evidence that integrated citizen service systems operate successfully nationwide. [s1]\n\nReading urban services through these distinctions produces a more useful picture: infrastructure is an input, service delivery is an activity, and better conditions for residents are an outcome. The inspected sources document policy ambitions, a Tehran strategy study, and a settlement-level intervention. They support that framework while leaving substantial gaps in current coverage and performance.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "National Urban Policy and Smart City Strategy: Summary Report",
